# 5. Rolling with fixed energy sharing.
P.append(mcq(5,'A rolling cylinder at a later time','A.4','ahl',r'A uniform solid cylinder rolls without slipping from rest down a straight incline. Its moment of inertia is $I=\tfrac12MR^2$. When its angular momentum about its centre has doubled, what is the factor by which its vertical drop has increased, and what fraction of its total kinetic energy is translational? The incline and $g$ are constant.', [r'$4$ and $2/3$',r'$2$ and $2/3$',r'$4$ and $1/2$',r'$2$ and $1/3$'],r'As $L=I\omega$, doubling $L$ doubles $\omega$ and, through $v=R\omega$, doubles $v$. Both kinetic-energy terms therefore quadruple, so the vertical drop quadruples by energy conservation. $K=\tfrac12Mv^2+\tfrac14Mv^2$, giving $K_\mathrm{trans}/K=2/3$.',C(8,4),'Rolling without slipping and rotational quantities','Combines a change in angular momentum with energy sharing rather than naming a time dependence.','Connect angular momentum, rolling kinematics and energy conservation.'))
