P.append(mcq(8,'A measured thermal-contact discontinuity','B.1','core',r'Two identical bars conduct heat steadily between reservoirs at $100^\circ\mathrm C$ and $0^\circ\mathrm C$. Each bar has thermal resistance $R$. An imperfect contact separates them: its hot-side and cold-side temperatures are measured as $60^\circ\mathrm C$ and $40^\circ\mathrm C$. For any element, a supplied model is $\Delta T=\dot Q R_{\rm th}$, where $\dot Q$ is the heat-transfer rate. The contact resistance is then doubled while bar resistances and reservoir temperatures stay fixed. What are the new-to-old heat-rate ratio and the new hot-side contact temperature?', [r'$5/6$ and $66.7^\circ\mathrm C$',r'$2/3$ and $66.7^\circ\mathrm C$',r'$5/6$ and $60.0^\circ\mathrm C$',r'$6/5$ and $33.3^\circ\mathrm C$'],r'Initially each bar has a $40\,\mathrm K$ drop, while the contact has $20\,\mathrm K$, so its resistance is $R/2$. Total resistance is $2.5R$. Doubling only the contact makes its resistance $R$ and the total $3R$, so $\dot Q_{\rm new}/\dot Q_{\rm old}=2.5/3=5/6$. The three equal new resistances divide the $100\,\mathrm K$ drop equally: the hot-side contact face is at $100-100/3=66.7^\circ\mathrm C$. The old contact temperatures do not remain fixed.',C(11,5),'Steady conduction through joined bars','Replaces a bare two-conductivity calculation by inferring an unknown interface resistance from a temperature discontinuity, then changing that contact.','Interpret a new supplied resistance model and recompute the entire temperature distribution after a controlled contact change.',d))
