# 11. Thermodynamic cycle: distinguish work input and temperature reduction.
P.append(mcq(11,'Reading a reversed ideal-gas cycle','B.4','ahl',r'A monatomic ideal gas begins at $A$ with pressure $p_0$ and volume $V_0$. It is compressed isothermally to $B$ at volume $V_0/2$, expands adiabatically to $C$ at volume $V_0$, and is heated at constant volume back to $A$. Use $pV^{5/3}=\text{constant}$ for an adiabat. What are the sign of the net work done by the gas and the ratio $T_C/T_A$?', [r'Negative; $2^{-2/3}$',r'Positive; $2^{-2/3}$',r'Negative; $2^{2/3}$',r'Positive; $2^{2/3}$'],r'At $B$, $p_B=2p_0$ and $T_B=T_A$. The adiabat gives $p_C=2p_0(1/2)^{5/3}=2^{-2/3}p_0$, hence $T_C/T_A=2^{-2/3}$ at equal volumes. Compression follows the higher-pressure isotherm and expansion the lower-pressure adiabat, so the negative compression work exceeds the positive expansion work: net work by the gas is negative.',C(14,7),'Isothermal compression, adiabatic expansion and isovolumetric heating','Reconstructs the cycle from states and asks for both the energy-direction and temperature consequence.','Link a PV path with the ideal-gas law and the sign of enclosed work.'))
