check('External-power ratio',(4/9)/(.5),8/9)
# 14. Velocity sign must be selected separately from its magnitude.
P.append(mcq(14,'A phase-sensitive oscillator state','C.1','ahl',r'An oscillator has displacement $x=0.060\cos(4t+\pi/3)$, with SI units. At an instant when $x=+0.030\,\mathrm m$ and the oscillator is moving in the positive direction, what are its velocity and acceleration?', [r'$+0.208\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ and $-0.480\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$',r'$-0.208\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ and $-0.480\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$',r'$+0.120\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ and $+0.480\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$',r'$+0.208\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ and $+0.480\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$'],r'$|v|=\omega\sqrt{A^2-x^2}=4\sqrt{0.060^2-0.030^2}=0.208\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. The stated motion selects the positive sign. Acceleration is $a=-\omega^2x=-16(0.030)=-0.480\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$, always towards equilibrium.',C(17,8),'Acceleration from sinusoidal SHM','Requires the correct velocity branch as well as acceleration at a nonzero displacement.','Use a speed–displacement relationship while preserving phase/sign information.'))
check('Positive oscillator velocity',4*math.sqrt(.06**2-.03**2),.2078460969,1e-10,units='m s^-1');check('Oscillator acceleration',-16*.03,-.48,units='m s^-2')
# 15. Both fields reverse after half a period; propagation does not.
d=svg(arrow(240,190,240,70)+text(240,50,'electric field')+circle(350,140,17)+line(340,130,360,150)+line(340,150,360,130)+text(420,145,'magnetic field')+text(350,185,'into the page')+arrow(90,250,500,250)+text(530,255,'+x'))
P.append(mcq(15,'An electromagnetic wave half a cycle later','C.2','core',r'At one instant, an electromagnetic wave has electric field upwards and magnetic field into the page, as shown. What are the magnetic-field direction half a period later and the propagation direction of the wave? Take right as positive $x$.', [r'Out of the page; to the left',r'Into the page; to the right',r'Out of the page; to the right',r'Into the page; to the left'],r'The electric and magnetic fields of the wave are in phase and both reverse after half a period. Thus the later magnetic field is out of the page. At either time, the mutually perpendicular field directions imply propagation to the left: initially upwards crossed with into the page points left. Reversing both fields leaves the propagation direction unchanged.',C(18,9),'Mutual perpendicularity and propagation of an EM wave','Adds a half-cycle evolution to the spatial field-direction inference.','Track phase changes without reversing the direction of energy propagation.',d))
# 16. Interface angles must be converted before reversing the ray.
d=svg(line(60,145,540,145)+line(300,35,300,270,dash=True)+arrow(127,45,300,145)+arrow(300,145,384,245)+text(250,135,'30°')+text(350,170,'50°')+text(465,70,'medium 1: n = 1.60')+text(460,235,'medium 2')+text(75,165,'interface',14,'start'))
P.append(mcq(16,'Reversing a refracted ray','C.3','core',r'A ray travels from medium 1 to medium 2. The labelled $30^\circ$ and $50^\circ$ are angles to the interface, not to the normal. The refractive index of medium 1 is $1.60$. What is the critical angle, measured from the normal, for light travelling from medium 2 to medium 1?', [r'$47.9^\circ$',r'$42.1^\circ$',r'$30.0^\circ$',r'$60.0^\circ$'],r'The normal angles are $60^\circ$ and $40^\circ$. Snell’s law gives $n_2=1.60\sin60^\circ/\sin40^\circ=2.16$. On reversing towards the lower-index medium, $\sin c=n_1/n_2=\sin40^\circ/\sin60^\circ$, so $c=47.9^\circ$. The complement is not the requested critical angle.',C(19,9),'Refraction angles measured to an interface','Uses the first refraction to infer an unknown index and then asks for the reverse critical angle.','Convert angle conventions and identify which direction permits total internal reflection.',d))
check('Unknown refractive index',1.6*math.sin(math.pi/3)/math.sin(math.radians(40)),2.1557,.0001);check('Critical angle',math.degrees(math.asin(math.sin(math.radians(40))/math.sin(math.pi/3))),47.9214859,1e-6,units='degree')
# 17. Missing maxima at the single-slit envelope boundary.
pts=[]
for i in range(901):
    theta=-.045+i*.0001;x=math.pi*theta/.040;sinc=1 if abs(x)<1e-12 else math.sin(x)/x
    pts.append((theta,(math.cos(math.pi*theta/.008)*sinc)**2))
d=graph(pts,(-.05,.05),(0,1.05),'diffraction angle / rad','Relative intensity',[(-.04,'−0.040'),(-.02,'−0.020'),(0,'0'),(.02,'0.020'),(.04,'0.040')],[(0,'0'),(1,'1')])
P.append(mcq(17,'Counting visible maxima inside an envelope','C.3','ahl',r'A double slit produces interference fringes with angular separation $0.0080\,\mathrm{rad}$. The first single-slit envelope minima are at $\pm0.040\,\mathrm{rad}$. Use the small-angle approximation. How many integer-order constructive-interference directions, including the central direction, lie strictly between those first envelope minima?', [r'$9$',r'$10$',r'$11$',r'$5$'],r'Integer constructive-interference orders occur at $\theta_m=m(0.0080)$. The envelope boundaries coincide with $m=\pm5$, where the intensity is zero, so these maxima are missing. The allowed indices strictly inside are $-4,-3,\ldots,3,4$, giving nine allowed integer orders including the central direction. The exact peaks of the combined envelope need not lie at these integer-order angles.',C(20,10),'Double-slit interference under a single-slit diffraction envelope','Asks for visible maxima with missing boundary orders rather than a direct slit-width ratio.','Combine two angular scales and handle the strict envelope boundary.',d))
check('Central-envelope bright maxima',len([k for k in range(-10,11) if abs(k*.008)<.04-1e-10]),9)
# 18. Two observed resonances need not include the fundamental.
d=svg(rect(90,80,360,90,'#f3f5f6')+line(90,80,90,170,width=7)+line(450,80,450,170,'white',width=7)+text(90,210,'closed')+text(450,210,'open')+text(270,115,'air column')+text(270,260,'Consecutive resonances: 210 Hz, 350 Hz'))
P.append(mcq(18,'The fundamental below two resonances','C.4','core',r'Two consecutive resonances of an air column closed at one end occur at $210\,\mathrm{Hz}$ and $350\,\mathrm{Hz}$. The speed of sound is $350\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$, and end corrections are negligible. What are the pipe length and the next resonance frequency above $350\,\mathrm{Hz}$?', [r'$1.25\,\mathrm m$ and $490\,\mathrm{Hz}$',r'$0.417\,\mathrm m$ and $630\,\mathrm{Hz}$',r'$0.625\,\mathrm m$ and $490\,\mathrm{Hz}$',r'$1.25\,\mathrm m$ and $420\,\mathrm{Hz}$'],r'A closed–open pipe has odd multiples of $f_0$, so consecutive resonances differ by $2f_0=140\,\mathrm{Hz}$. Hence $f_0=70\,\mathrm{Hz}$ and $L=v/(4f_0)=1.25\,\mathrm m$. The given resonances are $3f_0$ and $5f_0$; the next is $7f_0=490\,\mathrm{Hz}$.',C(21,11),'Allowed odd harmonics in a closed–open pipe','Uses two higher consecutive resonances to infer an unobserved fundamental and pipe length.','Infer harmonic indexing instead of assuming the lowest observed resonance is fundamental.',d))
check('Pipe length',350/(4*((350-210)/2)),1.25,units='m');check('Next resonance',350+(350-210),490,units='Hz')
# 19. Remove a wavelength zero offset before estimating recession speed.
P.append(mcq(19,'A calibrated stellar line shift','C.5','core',r'A stellar spectral line has laboratory wavelength $500.00\,\mathrm{nm}$. A spectrometer records the line at $500.35\,\mathrm{nm}$. A stationary calibration source shows that every wavelength reading is $0.10\,\mathrm{nm}$ too large. Using $c=3.00\times10^8\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ and the low-speed Doppler approximation, what is the star’s radial motion?', [r'Receding at $150\,\mathrm{km\,s^{-1}}$',r'Approaching at $150\,\mathrm{km\,s^{-1}}$',r'Receding at $210\,\mathrm{km\,s^{-1}}$',r'Approaching at $210\,\mathrm{km\,s^{-1}}$'],r'The corrected stellar wavelength is $500.25\,\mathrm{nm}$, so $\Delta\lambda/\lambda=0.25/500=5.0\times10^{-4}$. Hence $v=c\Delta\lambda/\lambda=1.50\times10^5\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. The corrected wavelength is still longer than the laboratory wavelength, so the star recedes. Using the uncorrected shift gives $210\,\mathrm{km\,s^{-1}}$.',C(22,11),'Redshift and relative spectral-line displacement','Adds a measured wavelength calibration offset before interpreting the shift.','Separate instrumental systematic error from an astrophysical signal.'))
check('Calibrated recession speed',3e8*((500.35-.10)-500)/500,150000,1e-6,units='m s^-1')
# 20. Distinguish two different single-moving-object Doppler experiments.
P.append(mcq(20,'Moving a source versus moving an observer','C.5','ahl',r'A horn has frequency $500\,\mathrm{Hz}$ and the speed of sound in still air is $330\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. In experiment I the horn approaches a stationary observer at $66\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. In a separate experiment II the horn is stationary and the observer approaches it at $66\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. What is the received frequency in experiment I minus that in experiment II?', [r'$25\,\mathrm{Hz}$',r'$0$',r'$125\,\mathrm{Hz}$',r'$100\,\mathrm{Hz}$'],r'A moving source compresses the wavelength: $f_I=fv/(v-u)=500(330)/(330-66)=625\,\mathrm{Hz}$. A moving observer meets unchanged wavefront spacing at a different rate: $f_{II}=f(v+u)/v=500(330+66)/330=600\,\mathrm{Hz}$. The difference is $25\,\mathrm{Hz}$. Sound’s stationary medium makes these experiments inequivalent; no experiment has both source and observer moving.',C(23,11),'Moving-source Doppler wavelength compression','Replaces a two-sided frequency-ratio inference by contrasting separate moving-source and moving-observer experiments at the same speed.','Select the correct single-moving-object Doppler relationship and explain the physical asymmetry.'))
check('Moving-source received frequency',500*330/(330-66),625,units='Hz');check('Moving-observer received frequency',500*(330+66)/330,600,units='Hz');check('Source-observer experiment difference',625-600,25,units='Hz')
# 21. Same density cancels the radius from a low circular-orbit period.
d=svg(circle(170,150,70,'#edf2f4')+circle(420,150,42,'#edf2f4')+line(170,150,240,150)+line(420,150,462,150)+text(170,245,'Earth: radius R')+text(420,245,'Planet: radius 0.60R')+text(300,30,'Equal average density; negligible atmospheres'))
P.append(mcq(21,'A small planet and a low orbit','D.1','core',r'A spherical planet has the same average density as Earth but radius $0.60$ times Earth’s radius. Atmospheric effects and rotation are negligible. What are the ratios of its surface gravitational field strength to Earth’s and of a very low circular-orbit period to the corresponding period about Earth?', [r'$0.60$ and $1$',r'$0.36$ and $0.60$',r'$1/0.60$ and $1$',r'$0.60$ and $\sqrt{0.60}$'],r'At fixed density, $M\propto R^3$, so $g=GM/R^2\propto R$ and the field ratio is $0.60$. From circular motion $T=2\pi\sqrt{R^3/(GM)}$, which is independent of radius at fixed density. Thus both very low orbit periods are equal.',C(24,11),'Density and radius scaling of surface gravity','Adds a circular-orbit comparison that cancels the radius dependence.','Combine density scaling with gravity and centripetal-motion models.',d))
check('Surface field ratio',.6**3/.6**2,.6);check('Low orbit period ratio',math.sqrt(.6**3/.6**3),1)
# 22. Zero resultant field does not mean zero potential.
d=svg(circle(90,145,15,'#edf2f4')+circle(500,145,30,'#edf2f4')+circle(192.5,145,4,'#17232b')+line(105,145,470,145,dash=True)+text(90,100,'m')+text(500,90,'9m')+text(192.5,120,'Z')+text(140,185,'r')+text(345,185,'3r')+text(300,245,'Objects isolated; potential is zero at infinity'))
P.append(mcq(22,'Energy at a gravitational field null','D.1','ahl',r'Two isolated point masses $m$ and $9m$ are separated by $4r$. A point $Z$ between them is distance $r$ from $m$, where the resultant gravitational field is zero. A small test mass $\mu$ is taken slowly from $Z$ to infinity. What work is done by the external agent?', [r'$+4Gm\mu/r$',r'$-4Gm\mu/r$',r'$0$',r'$+2Gm\mu/r$'],r'The field cancellation follows from $Gm/r^2=9Gm/(3r)^2$, but potentials add as scalars: $V_Z=-Gm/r-9Gm/(3r)=-4Gm/r$. Slow removal requires work $\Delta U=\mu(0-V_Z)=+4Gm\mu/r$. A zero local gradient does not make the potential zero.',C(25,12),'Two-mass gravitational-field null and nonzero potential','Changes from calculating potential to the signed work needed to remove a test mass.','Distinguish a vector field cancellation from scalar potential energy.',d))
check('Potential coefficient',-(1+9/3),-4)
# 23. Contact redistributes charge before the separation changes.
d=svg(circle(190,90,25)+circle(400,90,25)+text(190,95,'+3Q')+text(400,95,'−Q')+line(215,90,375,90,dash=True)+text(295,65,'r')+arrow(295,130,295,185)+circle(80,240,25)+circle(500,240,25)+text(80,245,'?')+text(500,245,'?')+line(105,240,475,240,dash=True)+text(290,220,'2r after contact'))
P.append(mcq(23,'Contact followed by a larger separation','D.2','core',r'Two identical conducting spheres initially have charges $+3Q$ and $-Q$, and their separation is $r$. The force magnitude is $F$. The spheres touch, are separated again, and are placed distance $2r$ apart. Their radii are negligible compared with either separation. What is the new force?', [r'$F/12$, repulsive',r'$F/3$, repulsive',r'$F/12$, attractive',r'$3F/4$, repulsive'],r'Total charge $+2Q$ divides equally, leaving $+Q$ on each sphere. Initially $F=3kQ^2/r^2$. Finally $F_f=kQ^2/(2r)^2=kQ^2/(4r^2)=F/12$, and equal positive charges repel. Both charge redistribution and the squared-distance change must be included.',C(26,12),'Charge sharing between identical conducting spheres','Changes initial charge ratio and final separation so two effects must be combined.','Apply charge conservation before using inverse-square force scaling.',d))
check('Final-to-initial electrostatic force',(1/4)/3,1/12)
# 24. Equipotential spacing and signed work.
d=svg(line(150,45,150,220)+line(300,45,300,220)+line(450,45,450,220)+text(150,30,'10 V')+text(300,30,'6 V')+text(450,30,'4 V')+text(150,245,'x = 1.0 cm')+text(300,245,'2.0 cm')+text(450,245,'3.0 cm')+arrow(100,275,520,275)+text(555,280,'+x'))
P.append(mcq(24,'Unequally spaced values on equally spaced surfaces','D.2','ahl',r'The diagram shows three equipotential surfaces in a region where the field is parallel to the $x$ axis. A charge $+2.0\,\mathrm{nC}$ moves from $x=1.0\,\mathrm{cm}$ to $x=3.0\,\mathrm{cm}$. What are the direction of the mean field in both intervals, the ratio of its mean magnitude in the first interval to that in the second, and the work done by the field on the charge?', [r'$+x$; $2$; $+12\,\mathrm{nJ}$',r'$-x$; $2$; $+12\,\mathrm{nJ}$',r'$+x$; $1/2$; $+12\,\mathrm{nJ}$',r'$+x$; $2$; $-12\,\mathrm{nJ}$'],r'The field points towards decreasing potential, so it is along $+x$. Equal interval widths contain potential drops of $4\,\mathrm V$ and $2\,\mathrm V$, giving mean field magnitudes in ratio $2:1$. The field work is $q(V_i-V_f)=2.0(10-4)=+12\,\mathrm{nJ}$.',C(27,13),'Interpretation of equipotential patterns','Uses measured equipotential values to infer two gradients and a signed energy transfer.','Connect a field to potential gradients while keeping work and potential change distinct.',d))
check('Mean-field ratio',((10-6)/.01)/((6-4)/.01),2);check('Work on positive charge',2*(10-4),12,units='nJ')
# 25. Exit displacement and exit angle scale differently.
d=svg(line(170,65,490,65)+line(170,215,490,215)+line(170,140,490,140,dash=True)+arrow(70,140,160,140)+text(85,115,'electron')+text(330,45,'uniform field')+arrow(520,95,520,185)+text(555,145,'E')+text(330,245,'plate length L'))
P.append(mcq(25,'Rescaling an electron deflection tube','D.3','core',r'An electron is accelerated from rest through a potential difference $V_a$, then enters midway between parallel plates with horizontal speed $v$. The uniform plate field is perpendicular to $v$, and the electron exits without hitting a plate. The accelerating potential is increased to $4V_a$ and the plate length is doubled. The plate field remains unchanged, and the electron still exits. How do its exit deflection and the tangent of its exit angle compare with the originals?', [r'Deflection unchanged; tangent halved',r'Deflection unchanged; tangent unchanged',r'Deflection halved; tangent halved',r'Deflection quartered; tangent halved'],r'Acceleration through $V_a$ gives $v\propto\sqrt{V_a}$, so the new horizontal speed is $2v$. Doubling the plate length leaves the transit time $L/v$ unchanged; hence the transverse velocity and deflection are unchanged. Since $\tan\theta=v_y/v_x$, doubling $v_x$ while retaining $v_y$ halves the tangent.',C(28,14),'Paths of electrons in a uniform electric field','Compares two tubes and distinguishes transverse displacement from exit direction.','Combine electrical acceleration with projectile-like component motion.',d))
check('Transit-time ratio',2/math.sqrt(4),1);check('Exit-tangent ratio',(2/math.sqrt(4))/math.sqrt(4),.5)
# 26. Antisymmetric forces create a couple.
d=svg(arrow(70,150,530,150)+arrow(300,35,300,265)+text(550,155,'X')+text(330,265,'Y')+text(185,125,'current →')+text(360,70,'current ↓')+circle(300,150,9,'white')+text(300,295,'Tiny insulated crossing; ignore its size'))
P.append(mcq(26,'A wire with no resultant force but a torque','D.3','ahl',r'A long vertical straight wire $Y$ carries current downwards. A finite horizontal wire $X$ carries current to the right and is centred on $Y$. Both are in the plane of the page; a tiny insulated crossing can be neglected. What are the resultant magnetic force on $X$ and its torque about the crossing, viewed as drawn?', [r'Zero force; clockwise torque',r'Zero force; anticlockwise torque',r'Upward force; zero torque',r'Downward force; zero torque'],r'For downward current in $Y$, its field is out of the page on the right and into the page on the left. With current in $X$ to the right, the right half experiences downward force and the left half upward force. Equal and opposite force contributions cancel, but both halves produce clockwise torque about the crossing.',C(29,14),'Resultant force and torque between perpendicular current-carrying wires','Requires the sense of a couple, not just whether force or torque is nonzero.','Apply magnetic-field and force directions to both symmetric halves.',d))
P[-1]['syllabusRefs']=['D.3','A.4']
# 27. Motional emf and the equilibrium charge separation.
d=svg(rect(270,65,22,160,'#edf2f4')+arrow(315,145,420,145)+text(480,160,'v')+text(280,45,'top')+text(280,250,'bottom')+''.join(line(x-4,y-4,x+4,y+4)+line(x-4,y+4,x+4,y-4) for x in [120,180,380,450] for y in [85,145,205] if not (y==145 and x>300))+text(140,280,'B into page'))
P.append(mcq(27,'Shortening a moving conductor','D.4','ahl',r'A vertical rod moves rightwards through a uniform magnetic field directed into the page. In open-circuit steady state, the magnitude of the potential difference between its ends is $\mathcal E$. Its speed is then tripled and its length halved, with the field unchanged. Which end is at higher potential, and what is the new potential-difference magnitude?', [r'Top; $1.5\mathcal E$',r'Bottom; $1.5\mathcal E$',r'Top; $6\mathcal E$',r'Bottom; $6\mathcal E$'],r'The force on positive charges is $q\mathbf v\times\mathbf B$, upwards here, so the top becomes positive and is at higher potential. Charge separation creates an opposing electric field. The open-circuit emf is $B\ell v$, and the new magnitude is $B(\ell/2)(3v)=1.5\mathcal E$.',C(30,15),'Electric and magnetic forces during motional induction','Combines the polarity with a compound geometry/speed change.','Connect charge separation, force balance and motional-emf scaling.',d))
check('Motional-emf ratio',3*.5,1.5)
# 28. Frequency changes together with emf amplitude.
P.append(mcq(28,'A rewound rotating-coil generator','D.4','ahl',r'A rectangular generator coil has $N$ turns, area $A$ per turn and rotation period $T$ in a constant uniform magnetic field. The normal to the coil rotates through all angles to the field. It is replaced by a coil with $2N$ turns and area $A/2$ per turn, rotating with period $T/2$. What are the ratios of peak induced emf and electrical frequency to their original values?', [r'$2$ and $2$',r'$1$ and $2$',r'$2$ and $1/2$',r'$4$ and $2$'],r'The peak induced emf is $NBA\omega$. The factors from turns, area and angular speed are $2$, $1/2$ and $2$, giving an overall factor of $2$. A complete revolution produces one complete sinusoidal cycle, so halving rotation period doubles the electrical frequency.',C(31,16),'Factors determining maximum induced emf in a rotating coil','Changes three generator properties and asks for both amplitude and frequency.','Separate flux-linkage amplitude from its rate of change.'))
check('Peak generator-emf ratio',2*.5*2,2);check('Generator frequency ratio',1/.5,2)
# 29. A supplied encounter model tests whether electrons could reverse alphas.
d=svg(arrow(50,145,230,145)+line(250,55,250,240,width=4)+arrow(250,145,510,145)+arrow(250,145,100,60)+text(130,175,'alpha beam')+text(275,45,'thin foil')+text(125,30,'rare backward event')+text(450,190,'most pass through'))
P.append(mcq(29,'Could one electron reverse an alpha particle?','E.1','core',r'Rare alpha particles reverse direction when a beam meets a thin metal foil. Test two possible single elastic encounters using a supplied head-on model: an alpha of mass $M$ and initial velocity $u$ meets a stationary target of mass $m$, leaving with velocity $v_\alpha=[(M-m)/(M+m)]u$. For an electron take $m=M/7000$; for a heavy nucleus take $m=50M$. Which approximate velocity pair and conclusion follow?', [r'Electron target: $+0.9997u$; nuclear target: $-0.961u$; only the heavy target can reverse the alpha',r'Electron target: $-0.9997u$; nuclear target: $+0.961u$; only the electron can reverse the alpha',r'Electron target: $+0.9997u$; nuclear target: $+0.961u$; neither can reverse the alpha',r'Electron target: $-0.9997u$; nuclear target: $-0.961u$; either can reverse the alpha'],r'For the electron target, $v_\alpha/u=(7000-1)/(7000+1)=+0.999714$: the alpha remains forward-moving. For the heavy target, $v_\alpha/u=(1-50)/(1+50)=-49/51=-0.960784$, so it reverses. The single-encounter model therefore rules out the much lighter electrons as the cause of strong alpha reversal; a sufficiently massive compact scattering region is required. The equation is supplied, so no simultaneous collision-energy/momentum derivation is required.',C(32,16),'Atomic-structure inference from rare alpha backscattering','Replaces a foil-thickness count scaling by a supplied two-body encounter test contrasting electrons with a heavy nuclear target.','Use mass-ratio limits and the sign of a supplied velocity relation to evaluate competing atomic-scattering explanations.',d))
check('Electron-target retained alpha velocity factor',(7000-1)/(7000+1),.9997143265247822,1e-12);check('Nucleus-target alpha velocity factor',(1-50)/(1+50),-.9607843137254902,1e-12)
# 30. Distinguish cascade and direct emission.
d=svg(line(100,60,470,60)+line(100,125,470,125)+line(100,255,470,255)+text(525,65,'−0.50 eV')+text(525,130,'−2.00 eV')+text(525,260,'−5.00 eV')+arrow(180,65,180,120)+text(155,100,'P')+arrow(300,130,300,250)+text(275,195,'Q')+arrow(410,65,410,250)+text(440,175,'R'))
P.append(mcq(30,'A cascade beside a direct atomic transition','E.1','core',r'The atom has the energy levels and downward transitions shown. Take visible wavelengths as $400$–$700\,\mathrm{nm}$ and use $hc=1240\,\mathrm{eV\,nm}$. Which transition emits visible light, and which emits ultraviolet light?', [r'Q visible; R ultraviolet',r'P visible; Q ultraviolet',r'R visible; P ultraviolet',r'Q visible; P ultraviolet'],r'The photon energies are $E_P=1.50\,\mathrm{eV}$, $E_Q=3.00\,\mathrm{eV}$ and $E_R=4.50\,\mathrm{eV}$. Their wavelengths are approximately $827$, $413$ and $276\,\mathrm{nm}$. Thus P is infrared, Q visible and R ultraviolet. A two-photon cascade conserves the same total energy as R but does not emit a single R-frequency photon.',C(33,17),'Visible and UV emission from atomic energy levels','Uses a three-level cascade with new energies and explicit spectral boundaries.','Translate level differences into wavelengths rather than comparing absolute level energies.',d))
check('P infrared wavelength',1240/1.5,826.6666667,1e-6,units='nm');check('Q visible wavelength',1240/3,413.3333333,1e-6,units='nm');check('R ultraviolet wavelength',1240/4.5,275.5555556,1e-6,units='nm')
# 31. Compton geometry and electron energy follow from different conservation laws.
P.append(mcq(31,'A large Compton wavelength shift','E.2','ahl',r'A photon of wavelength $\lambda_C=h/(m_ec)$ is scattered by a stationary electron. Its wavelength increases by $1.5\lambda_C$. Use $\Delta\lambda=\lambda_C(1-\cos\theta)$ and neglect binding effects. What are the photon scattering angle and the fraction of the incident photon energy transferred to the electron?', [r'$120^\circ$ and $0.60$',r'$60^\circ$ and $0.60$',r'$120^\circ$ and $1.50$',r'$60^\circ$ and $0.40$'],r'$1-\cos\theta=1.5$ gives $\cos\theta=-0.5$, so $\theta=120^\circ$. The scattered wavelength is $2.5\lambda_C$, making the photon energy $1/2.5=0.40$ of its initial value. Energy conservation transfers the remaining fraction $0.60$ to electron kinetic energy. The wavelength increase is not an energy fraction.',C(34,18),'Compton wavelength shift and scattering geometry','Requires both angle inference and a reciprocal-wavelength energy budget.','Use the wavelength-shift relation and energy conservation without conflating them.'))
check('Compton scattering angle',math.degrees(math.acos(1-1.5)),120,1e-10,units='degree');check('Photon-to-electron energy fraction',1-1/2.5,.6)
# 32. Invert a wavelength change to recover an electrical energy loss.
P.append(mcq(32,'Retarding an electron beam without stopping it','E.2','ahl',r'Electrons start from rest and are accelerated through $900\,\mathrm V$. They then pass through a retarding electric potential difference without stopping. Their de Broglie wavelength after retardation is $50\%$ greater than immediately before it. Speeds are non-relativistic. What is the magnitude of the retarding potential difference?', [r'$500\,\mathrm V$',r'$300\,\mathrm V$',r'$400\,\mathrm V$',r'$600\,\mathrm V$'],r'Non-relativistically $\lambda=h/\sqrt{2mK}$, so $K_f/K_i=(\lambda_i/\lambda_f)^2=(1/1.5)^2=4/9$. Initially $K_i=900\,\mathrm{eV}$; afterwards it is $400\,\mathrm{eV}$. The electric field therefore removes $500\,\mathrm{eV}$ per electron, corresponding to a retarding potential magnitude of $500\,\mathrm V$. The wavelength increase is not the fractional energy loss.',C(35,18),'de Broglie wavelength scaling with kinetic energy','Replaces a two-particle charge/mass comparison by an inverse measurement of retarding voltage from the same electron beam’s wavelength change.','Square and invert a measured wavelength ratio before applying electrical work.'))
check('Retarded electron kinetic-energy fraction',(1/1.5)**2,4/9);check('Retarded electron kinetic energy',900*(1/1.5)**2,400,1e-10,units='eV');check('Retarding voltage magnitude',900-900*(1/1.5)**2,500,1e-10,units='V')
# 33. A real multi-step chain returns to the original proton number.
d=svg(rect(30,100,100,65,'#edf2f4')+text(80,139,'²³⁸₉₂U')+arrow(135,130,210,130)+text(170,105,'α')+rect(215,100,90,65)+text(260,139,'X')+arrow(310,130,385,130)+text(345,105,'β⁻')+rect(390,100,70,65)+text(425,139,'Y')+arrow(465,130,530,130)+text(495,105,'β⁻')+text(560,139,'Z'))
P.append(mcq(33,'A short uranium decay chain','E.3','core',r'A uranium nucleus $^{238}_{92}\mathrm U$ undergoes one alpha decay followed by two beta-minus decays. Gamma emissions, if any, can be ignored. How many protons and neutrons are in the final nucleus Z?', [r'$92$ protons and $142$ neutrons',r'$90$ protons and $144$ neutrons',r'$94$ protons and $140$ neutrons',r'$92$ protons and $146$ neutrons'],r'Alpha decay changes $(A,Z)$ from $(238,92)$ to $(234,90)$. Each beta-minus decay converts a neutron to a proton, increasing Z by one without changing A. The final values are $A=234$, $Z=92$, and $N=A-Z=142$. Gamma emission changes neither.',C(36,19),'Proton and neutron accounting in alpha decay','Uses a physically realizable three-step chain with competing alpha and beta changes.','Track nucleon and proton numbers separately through successive decays.',d))
check('Final proton number',92-2+2,92);check('Final neutron number',(238-4)-(92-2+2),142)
# 34. Beta-plus composition together with an energy-sharing event.
P.append(mcq(34,'Composition and missing energy in beta-plus decay','E.3','core',r'A $^{13}_{7}\mathrm N$ nucleus undergoes beta-plus decay. The available kinetic energy is $1.20\,\mathrm{MeV}$. In one event the positron receives $0.70\,\mathrm{MeV}$; daughter recoil can be neglected. What are the daughter proton number and the energy carried by the electron neutrino?', [r'$6$ and $0.50\,\mathrm{MeV}$',r'$8$ and $0.50\,\mathrm{MeV}$',r'$6$ and $1.90\,\mathrm{MeV}$',r'$8$ and $1.20\,\mathrm{MeV}$'],r'In beta-plus decay a proton becomes a neutron while a positron and an electron neutrino are emitted, so the daughter has Z=6 and unchanged A=13. Energy conservation leaves $1.20-0.70=0.50\,\mathrm{MeV}$ for the neutrino when recoil is neglected. The variable sharing is why beta energies are continuous.',C(37,19),'Nuclear changes in beta-plus decay','Adds a specific beta energy-sharing event to daughter-nucleus accounting.','Combine nuclear charge conservation with the neutrino energy balance.'))
check('Neutrino energy',1.20-.70,.50,units='MeV')
# 35. Finite-time decay probability is not lambda times a long interval.
P.append(mcq(35,'When a small-time decay approximation fails','E.3','ahl',r'A nuclide has half-life $18\,\mathrm h$. What is the probability that a particular undecayed nucleus decays in the next $6.0\,\mathrm h$, and how does $\lambda\Delta t$ compare with that probability?', [r'$0.206$; $\lambda\Delta t$ is larger',r'$0.231$; $\lambda\Delta t$ is exactly equal',r'$0.794$; $\lambda\Delta t$ is smaller',r'$0.333$; $\lambda\Delta t$ is larger'],r'The survival probability is $e^{-\lambda\Delta t}=2^{-6/18}$, so the decay probability is $1-2^{-1/3}=0.206$. Here $\lambda\Delta t=(\ln2)/3=0.231$, which overestimates the probability. The linear expression is only a small-interval approximation; surviving population decreases throughout the interval.',C(38,19),'Probability per unit time and decay constant','Replaces a one-second approximation with a finite fraction of a half-life and asks about approximation bias.','Distinguish survival, decay probability and a small-time linear approximation.'))
check('Six-hour decay probability',1-2**(-1/3),.2062994740,1e-9);check('Linear approximation',math.log(2)/3,.2310490602,1e-9)
# 36. A supplied collision model makes the moderator choice quantitative.
P.append(mcq(36,'Quantifying an effective moderator','E.4','core',r'For a head-on elastic collision between a neutron and a stationary nucleus of mass $Am_n$, a supplied model gives neutron energy ratio $K_f/K_i=[(A-1)/(A+1)]^2$. A neutron makes two such collisions with nuclei of $A=2$. Which remaining-energy fraction and material property favour effective moderation?', [r'$1/81$; stable nuclei with a low neutron-absorption probability',r'$1/9$; nuclei with a high neutron-absorption probability',r'$1/81$; unstable nuclei with a high neutron-absorption probability',r'$8/9$; stable nuclei with a low neutron-absorption probability'],r'One collision leaves $[(2-1)/(2+1)]^2=1/9$ of the neutron energy; two leave $(1/9)^2=1/81$. Moderation should slow neutrons while retaining them for the chain reaction, so stability and a low absorption probability are desirable. Absorbing neutrons is primarily the role of control materials, not the required moderation mechanism.',C(39,19),'Desirable moderator mass and stability','Supplies a collision law to require an energy-fraction calculation and distinguish scattering from absorption.','Apply an unfamiliar stated relation twice and interpret reactor function.'))
check('Two-collision energy fraction',(((2-1)/(2+1))**2)**2,1/81)
# 37. A detector separation is twice the parallax angle.
