P.append(written('1B',2,'Testing a closest-approach model','E.1','ahl',r'A research-training simulation produces synthetic data for head-on alpha scattering from heavy stationary nuclei. Outside the nucleus, the purely electrostatic model predicts $d=K/E$ and $K=2Z(1.44\,\mathrm{MeV\,fm})$, where $E$ is incident alpha kinetic energy, $d$ is closest approach, and $Z$ is target proton number. Binding and target recoil are neglected. Each $d$ has uncertainty $\pm0.5\,\mathrm{fm}$; energy uncertainty is negligible. The reciprocal-energy graph represents these data.'+'\n\n'+r'| $E/\mathrm{MeV}$ | 3.00 | 4.00 | 5.00 | 6.00 | 8.00 |'+'\n'+r'|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|'+'\n'+r'| $d/\mathrm{fm}$ | 33.6 | 25.2 | 20.16 | 16.8 | 12.6 |',[
 ('a',r'Explain why the model predicts a straight line through the origin on these axes.',1),
 ('b',r'Use the $3.00$ and $8.00\,\mathrm{MeV}$ endpoints to calculate $K$ and its uncertainty from the steepest and shallowest endpoint lines.',2),
 ('c',r'Calculate the target proton number and its uncertainty implied by part (b).',2),
 ('d',r'The target nucleon number is $80$ and its radius is modelled by $R=1.20A^{1/3}\,\mathrm{fm}$. Estimate the incident energy at which electrostatic closest approach would equal this radius.',2),
 ('e',r'At $12.0\,\mathrm{MeV}$ a new simulation gives $d=(6.0\pm0.5)\,\mathrm{fm}$. Test its consistency with the electrostatic prediction using your uncertainty in $K$. State one justified limitation of interpreting the discrepancy as proof of a particular nuclear mechanism.',3)], [
 ('a',[('R1',r'Writing $x=1/E$ gives $d=Kx$: a constant gradient K and zero intercept. A nonzero intercept is not predicted by this model.')]),
 ('b',[('A1',r'$K=(33.6-12.6)/(1/3-1/8)=100.8\,\mathrm{MeV\,fm}$.'),('A1',r'$K_{\max}=(34.1-12.1)/(1/3-1/8)=105.6$ and $K_{\min}=(33.1-13.1)/(1/3-1/8)=96.0\,\mathrm{MeV\,fm}$, giving $K=(100.8\pm4.8)\,\mathrm{MeV\,fm}$.')]),
 ('c',[('M1',r'$Z=K/(2\times1.44)$ and the same constant divisor applies to $\Delta Z$.'),('A1',r'$Z=35.0$ with $\Delta Z=4.8/2.88=1.7$. Treating Z as physically integral does not remove the measurement uncertainty; the data are consistent with proton number 35.')]),
 ('d',[('M1',r'$R=1.20(80)^{1/3}=5.17\,\mathrm{fm}$ and $E=K/R$.'),('A1',r'$E\approx100.8/5.17=19.5\,\mathrm{MeV}$. This is an estimate of the geometric scale at which the outside-point-charge model ceases to be reliable, not a guaranteed exact onset of a nuclear effect.')]),
 ('e',[('A1',r'The old model predicts $d=100.8/12.0=8.40\,\mathrm{fm}$, with $\Delta d=4.8/12.0=0.40\,\mathrm{fm}$.'),('R1',r'The predicted interval $8.0$–$8.8\,\mathrm{fm}$ does not overlap $5.5$–$6.5\,\mathrm{fm}$, so the new result is inconsistent within the stated uncertainties.'),('R1',r'The disagreement alone does not establish a unique mechanism: an unmodelled systematic distance/energy bias, finite charge distribution or neglected target response must be checked. Accept another specific, scientifically justified model or calibration limitation; “human error” alone is insufficient.')])],('49.pdf',12,7),'Coulomb closest approach, nuclear radius and breakdown of Rutherford modelling','Recasts a single-target calculation as a full synthetic reciprocal-energy dataset, uncertainty propagation and explicit model-failure test.','Infer a model parameter from a transformed graph, propagate endpoint uncertainty and distinguish inconsistency from a unique causal conclusion.',d,refs=['E.1','D.2','Tools']))
K=(33.6-12.6)/(1/3-1/8);dK=((34.1-12.1)-(33.1-13.1))/(1/3-1/8)/2
check('Scattering graph slope',K,100.8,1e-10,units='MeV fm');check('Scattering slope uncertainty',dK,4.8,1e-10,units='MeV fm');check('Target proton number',K/2.88,35,1e-10);check('Proton-number uncertainty',dK/2.88,1.66666666666667,1e-10);check('Target nuclear radius',1.2*80**(1/3),5.17064325607652,1e-10,units='fm');check('Geometric contact-energy estimate',K/(1.2*80**(1/3)),19.494673101173678,1e-9,units='MeV');check('New predicted closest approach',K/12,8.4,1e-10,units='fm');check('New predicted distance uncertainty',dK/12,.4,1e-10,units='fm');evidence('Predicted [8.0,8.8] fm and observed [5.5,6.5] fm do not overlap; that establishes failure of the stated model/error budget without identifying a unique cause.')
# Paper 2: nine linked ten-mark problems, all independently worded and recalculated.
