Q.append(written('2',2,'Freezing water without cooling the final mixture','B.4','ahl',r'Ice of mass $0.180\,\mathrm{kg}$ at $250\,\mathrm K$ is put into a large stirred ice–water mixture at $273\,\mathrm K$. The combined system is insulated, the container heat capacity is negligible, and enough liquid water remains that final equilibrium is at $273\,\mathrm K$. Use ice specific heat capacity $c=2100\,\mathrm{J\,kg^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$ and latent heat of fusion $L_f=3.34\times10^5\,\mathrm{J\,kg^{-1}}$. For a solid with constant $c$, the entropy change between temperatures is supplied as $\Delta S=mc\ln(T_f/T_i)$.',[
 ('a',r'Determine the mass of liquid water that freezes.',2),
 ('b',r'Explain how the freezing water loses internal energy while its temperature remains constant.',2),
 ('c',r'Calculate the entropy increase of the initially cold ice.',2),
 ('d',r'Calculate the total entropy change of the combined system and explain its consistency with the second law.',2),
 ('e',r'In a repeat, the same ice mass starts at $270\,\mathrm K$. Calculate the new total entropy change and explain why it differs from part (d).',2)], [
 ('a',[('M1',r'The cold ice requires $Q=mc(273-250)=0.180(2100)(23)=8694\,\mathrm J$. The freezing water supplies this latent energy.'),('A1',r'$m_f=Q/L_f=8694/(3.34\times10^5)=0.0260\,\mathrm{kg}$.')]),
 ('b',[('R1',r'Temperature reflects mean random kinetic energy, which does not decrease during the equilibrium phase change.'),('R1',r'Intermolecular potential energy decreases as the solid structure forms; the released latent energy warms the initially colder ice. Thus constant temperature does not imply constant internal energy.')]),
 ('c',[('M1',r'Apply the supplied relationship to the warming ice, not $Q/273$ for the whole varying-temperature process.'),('A1',r'$\Delta S_\mathrm{ice}=0.180(2100)\ln(273/250)=33.3\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$.')]),
 ('d',[('A1',r'The water freezes at $273\,\mathrm K$, so $\Delta S_\mathrm{water}=-8694/273=-31.8\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$. Total entropy increases by about $1.42\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$.'),('R1',r'The isolated combined system has positive entropy production from energy transfer across a finite temperature difference; one component may lose entropy while the total increases.')]),
 ('e',[('A1',r'$\Delta S_\mathrm{total}=0.180(2100)[\ln(273/270)-3/273]\approx0.0230\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$.'),('R1',r'The initial temperature difference is much smaller, so the energy transfer is closer to reversible and produces less entropy. Both totals remain positive.')])],('49.pdf',4,3),'Ice warming by freezing water and entropy accounting','Supplies the varying-temperature entropy expression, introduces a comparison repeat and explicitly defines the isolated system.','Distinguish thermal kinetic and potential energy and compare irreversible entropy budgets.',d,refs=['B.1','B.4']))
