Q.append(written('2',6,'How far outside the tin nucleus?','E.1','ahl',r'An alpha particle approaches a stationary tin nucleus head-on with initial kinetic energy $6.00\,\mathrm{MeV}$ when far away. The tin proton number is $50$, and its nucleon number is $120$. Neglect target recoil and assume electrostatic repulsion outside the nucleus. Use $ke^2=1.44\,\mathrm{MeV\,fm}$ and $R=1.20A^{1/3}\,\mathrm{fm}$.',[
 ('a',r'Determine the distance of closest approach between the nuclear centres.',2),
 ('b',r'Calculate the tin nuclear radius and the ratio of closest approach to this radius.',2),
 ('c',r'The target is replaced by another tin isotope of nucleon number $125$. Explain how the predicted closest approach and nuclear radius change at the same incident alpha energy.',2),
 ('d',r'Estimate the alpha energy for which the electrostatic closest approach would equal the radius of the original $A=120$ nucleus.',2),
 ('e',r'Explain two reasons why the point-charge electrostatic description may fail once the closest-approach distance becomes comparable with the nuclear radius.',2)], [
 ('a',[('M1',r'At the head-on turning point, relative kinetic energy is zero and $K=k(2e)(50e)/d$.'),('A1',r'$d=2(50)(1.44)/6.00=24.0\,\mathrm{fm}$. This is centre-to-centre distance.')]),
 ('b',[('A1',r'$R=1.20(120)^{1/3}=5.92\,\mathrm{fm}$.'),('A1',r'$d/R=24.0/5.92=4.05$.')]),
 ('c',[('R1',r'Closest approach is unchanged in the stated no-recoil model because both isotopes have Z=50 and the incident energy is unchanged.'),('R1',r'Radius increases by $(125/120)^{1/3}=1.014$, about $1.4\%$, because nuclear radius depends on nucleon number.')]),
 ('d',[('M1',r'Set $d=R$ in the electrostatic energy relation, giving $K_\mathrm{est}=2Zke^2/R$.'),('A1',r'$K_\mathrm{est}=144/5.92=24.3\,\mathrm{MeV}$. It is an estimate of a scale, not an exact threshold for every nuclear effect.')]),
 ('e',[('R1',r'The target charge occupies a finite volume, so when the alpha penetrates that volume the outside point-charge potential no longer applies.'),('R1',r'At nuclear separations the short-range strong nuclear interaction becomes important and electrostatic repulsion alone is insufficient. Accept a specific valid discussion of nuclear response/reactions; do not award a second mark for merely repeating that nuclei have a size.')])],('49.pdf',12,7),'Closest approach, isotope dependence, nuclear radius and high-energy departures','Uses tin and two isotope masses, states no recoil explicitly and asks about distinct physical limits of the Coulomb model.','Separate proton-number and nucleon-number dependences and interpret a model’s validity range.',d,refs=['E.1','D.2']))
