Q.append(written('2',7,'The turning point of an elastic safety cord','A.3','core',r'A $2.00\,\mathrm{kg}$ test mass is released from rest at the fixed end of a light elastic safety cord. The cord is slack until the mass has fallen its unstretched length $1.60\,\mathrm m$. It then obeys Hooke’s law and reaches maximum extension $0.800\,\mathrm m$. Energy losses are negligible; use $g=10.0\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$. A timer measures $0.205\,\mathrm s$ from the cord first becoming taut at A to the mass stopping at B.',[
 ('a',r'Calculate the speed at A.',2),
 ('b',r'Determine the cord’s spring constant.',2),
 ('c',r'Determine the magnitude and direction of the acceleration at B. Explain why zero speed does not imply equilibrium.',2),
 ('d',r'Use impulse to calculate the average upward cord force between A and B.',2),
 ('e',r'A student estimates this average force as one half of the maximum cord force. Test that estimate and explain why a linear force–extension law does not justify it.',2)], [
 ('a',[('M1',r'Before A the mass falls freely, so $v_A^2=2gL$.'),('A1',r'$v_A=\sqrt{2(10.0)(1.60)}=5.66\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ downwards.')]),
 ('b',[('M1',r'The full fall to B is $L+x=2.40\,\mathrm m$, so $mg(L+x)=\tfrac12kx^2$.'),('A1',r'$k=2(2.00)(10.0)(2.40)/(0.800)^2=150\,\mathrm{N\,m^{-1}}$.')]),
 ('c',[('A1',r'At B, $F_\mathrm{cord}=kx=120\,\mathrm N$ upward, giving resultant force $120-20=100\,\mathrm N$ and acceleration $50.0\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$ upward.'),('R1',r'B is an instantaneous turning point. Its velocity is zero but the forces do not balance, so it is not an equilibrium state.')]),
 ('d',[('M1',r'Take upward positive: $(\overline F_\mathrm{cord}-mg)\Delta t=m[0-(-v_A)]$.'),('A1',r'$\overline F_\mathrm{cord}=20.0+2.00(5.6569)/0.205=75.2\,\mathrm N$. Accept a consistent follow-through from part (a).')]),
 ('e',[('A1',r'Half the maximum force is $60.0\,\mathrm N$, which does not match the impulse-derived $75.2\,\mathrm N$.'),('R1',r'Hooke’s law makes force linear in extension, not linear in time. The changing velocity means extensions do not occupy equal time intervals, so the time average is not the midpoint of the force range.')])],('49.pdf',14,8),'Free fall onto an elastic rope, energy and average tension','Changes mass/length/extension, supplies a dynamically consistent timing measurement and tests an incorrect time-average argument.','Use whole-journey energy, turning-point dynamics and impulse without confusing time and extension averages.',d,refs=['A.2','A.3']))
